SINGLY LINKED LIST
==================================================

1. What is Linked List?
	A Linked List is a linear data structure in which elements are stored in the form of nodes. Each node contains two parts: data and a reference to the next node.
	
	- Elements are not stored in continuous memory locations.
	- Each node is connected to the next node using a reference.
	- Linked List has a dynamic size, meaning we can add or remove nodes at runtime.


NOTE
	-Whenever a new Node is created, its  next reference is null. 
	- We establish connections between nodes after  the new Node is created.
	
	
	

- head ,next and tail are reference variables that store the reference  address of of Node objects.

- When we compare two Node references using ==,
  Java compares their references (whether they point
  to the same object), not their data values.


2. Example
	Node n1 = new Node(10);
	Node n2 = new Node(20);
	
		head = n1;
	tail = n2;
	n1.next = n2;


3. Memory Representation

        REFERENCE VARIABLES

        head  --------->  1001

        tail  --------->  2002


        HEAP MEMORY

     Address: 1001

    +------------------+
    | data = 10        |
    | next = 2002      |
    +------------------+
             |
             | Reference
             v
     Address: 2002

    +------------------+
    | data = 20        |
    | next = null      |
    +------------------+


NOTE:

- head stores the reference of the first Node (1001).
- tail stores the reference of the last Node (2002).
- n1.next stores the reference of the next Node (2002).
- The last Node's next contains null because there
  is no next Node.
- This representation helps us understand how head,
  tail and next maintain connections between nodes.

==> important for understanding whole linkedList concept what head ,tail,next stores 


4. Reference Comparison Using ==

	head(1001) == n1(1001);       // true

	tail(2002) == n2(2002);       // true

	head(1001) == tail(2002);     // false

	n1.next(2002) == n2(2002);    // true


EXPLANATION:

- head == n1 returns true because both refer to
  the same Node object (1001).

- head == tail returns false because they refer
  to different Node objects (1001 and 2002).

- n1.next == n2 returns true because both refer
  to the same Node object (2002).


5. Important Points to Remember

- == compares object references, not data values.

- Two reference variables can point to the same
  Node object.

- Different reference variables can store the
  reference of the same Node object.

- next maintains the connection between two nodes.

- head maintains the reference of the first node.

- tail maintains the reference of the last node.

- The last node's next always contains null.

